# mackas-mirrord.conf -- configuration for mackas-mirrord
#
# Copyright (C) 2026 Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# Point at it with --config FILE, or $MACKAS_MIRRORD_CONFIG.
#
# This file is KEY=VALUE and is NOT sourced as shell -- unlike mackas.conf,
# which is. A daemon that exec'd its own config file would turn "can write the
# config" into "can run code as the daemon user", and this file lives in /etc
# next to a credential. No expansion, no substitution, no commands.
#
# PRECEDENCE, lowest to highest (the same convention as mackas):
#
#   built-in defaults  ->  this file  ->  environment  ->  command-line flags
#
# Every key here is also an environment variable named MACKAS_MIRRORD_<KEY>,
# and a command-line flag named --<key-with-dashes>.


# --- What to serve -----------------------------------------------------------

# Comma-separated NAME=PATH. Each is served under /NAME/.
#
# One process serving both caches on one port, rather than two processes on
# two ports: one privilege drop, one TLS context, one credential store, one
# unit to harden. bitbake does not care -- a path prefix distinguishes the two
# mirrors as well as a port number does.
#
# Example: the caches of a build tree on the mirror host.
ROOTS=sstate=/build/angstrom-2026.06/build/sstate-cache,downloads=/build/angstrom-2026.06/build/downloads

# Directory listings. OFF unless you say otherwise, and you should not: a
# listing of an sstate cache is a map of every package, version and hash the
# mirror host has ever built. bitbake never reads one -- it constructs exact
# object URLs from its own hashes.
#LISTING=0

# Refuse ALL symlinks under the root, not just escaping ones.
#
# Escaping symlinks are ALWAYS refused, regardless of this setting -- that is
# the non-negotiable part, because the root is an NFS mount whose contents we
# do not control, and anyone who can write to the export could otherwise drop
# in a link to /etc/shadow and read it through us.
#
# This setting additionally refuses symlinks that stay inside the root. Off by
# default because DL_DIR legitimately contains them and refusing would break
# real fetches. Turn it on for an sstate-only root, where there are none.
#NO_SYMLINKS=0


# --- Local cache ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Off by default -- this is an opt-in accelerator, not a default-on cache.
# Set it to enable: once an object has been requested more than
# CACHE_MIN_HITS - 1 times today, it is copied into this directory in the
# background and later requests are served from there instead of the
# (possibly slow) NFS/SMB root. See docs/mirror-server.md for the full
# design, including why this is the daemon's ONLY write path and how it
# stays safe.
#
# Must be OUTSIDE every ROOTS path, in both directions. Created (mode 0700)
# if it does not exist; refused at startup if it is group- or
# world-writable, for the same reason the credential file is -- this is the
# server's write path, and anyone who can write here can plant a file that
# gets served as though it were a legitimate cache hit.
#CACHE_DIR=/var/cache/mackas-mirrord

# Promote to the cache after this many requests for the same object in one
# calendar day. Default 3, i.e. "more than 2": the 1st and 2nd requests are
# misses, the 3rd triggers a background copy.
#CACHE_MIN_HITS=3

# Evict least-recently-used cached objects once the cache exceeds this size,
# in MiB. This is the backstop for a SINGLE day -- daily rotation (purging
# yesterday's entries, not configurable, always on when caching is on) is
# what actually keeps steady-state size down. 4096 (4 GiB) comfortably fits
# a few dozen to a few hundred sstate objects without asking an operator who
# just wants "cache the hot ones" to provision real storage.
#CACHE_MAX_SIZE_MB=4096


# --- Where to listen ---------------------------------------------------------

# The default is 8100.
#PORT=8100

# 0.0.0.0 binds every interface. Narrow it if the host is multi-homed.
#BIND=0.0.0.0


# --- Access control ----------------------------------------------------------

# IP allowlist, comma- or space-separated CIDRs. Host bits are tolerated
# ("192.0.2.5/24" means the /24).
#
# On a trusted wired LAN this is a legitimate control ON ITS OWN, and running
# allowlist-only is supported: no password to distribute, nothing to leak, and
# the cache contents are build artefacts rather than secrets. It is NOT a
# control on a network where anyone can spoof or where the "LAN" includes
# guest wifi.
ALLOW=192.0.2.0/24

# Basic auth. Generate the record (it prompts; the password never appears on
# argv, which /proc/PID/cmdline shows to every user on the box):
#
#   mackas-mirrord --hash-password > /etc/mackas-mirrord.cred
#   chmod 600 /etc/mackas-mirrord.cred
#
# The file stores a salted PBKDF2-SHA256 hash, not the password. The daemon
# REFUSES to start if the file is group- or world-accessible.
#CRED_FILE=/etc/mackas-mirrord.cred

#REALM=mackas mirror


# --- TLS ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Off by default. Be clear-eyed about the trade:
#
#   Basic auth over plain HTTP sends the password in the clear. base64 is an
#   encoding, not encryption -- anyone on the path reads it. On a trusted LAN
#   with an IP allowlist that is an acceptable risk and this is why ALLOW
#   above exists. Anywhere else, turn TLS on.
#
# The stdlib ssl module can load certs but not conveniently create them, so a
# self-signed cert needs the openssl CLI. The exact command:
#
#   openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -nodes -days 3650 \
#       -keyout /etc/mackas-mirrord.key -out /etc/mackas-mirrord.crt \
#       -subj "/CN=linux-computer" \
#       -addext "subjectAltName=DNS:linux-computer,IP:192.0.2.10"
#   chmod 600 /etc/mackas-mirrord.key
#
# A self-signed cert means clients must either trust it explicitly or skip
# verification -- and a client skipping verification gets no protection from
# an active attacker, only from a passive one. If that matters, use a real CA.
#
# Minimum protocol version is TLS 1.2, not configurable. Nothing older is
# worth supporting.
#TLS_CERT=/etc/mackas-mirrord.crt
#TLS_KEY=/etc/mackas-mirrord.key


# --- Hardening ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Drop to this user/group if started as root. The daemon REFUSES to run as
# root without one (or an explicit ALLOW_ROOT=1), because nothing it does
# needs privilege. The systemd unit sets User= itself, so these are for
# running it by hand.
#USER=nobody
#GROUP=nogroup
#ALLOW_ROOT=0

# Per-connection socket timeout, in seconds. This is the slowloris defence: a
# client dribbling one byte a minute gets evicted after this long instead of
# holding a thread forever.
#TIMEOUT=30

# Hard cap on concurrent connections. Past it, new connections are closed
# immediately rather than degrading things for everyone. ThreadingHTTPServer
# alone would spawn a thread per connection until the box died.
#MAX_CONNECTIONS=64

# Per-IP token bucket: requests/second, and the burst allowance. 0 disables.
#
# Off by default, and honestly so: bitbake legitimately fires thousands of
# sstate probes as fast as it can, and a limit tuned to stop an attacker would
# also throttle the one client this exists to serve. Turn it on only if you
# expose this beyond a LAN, and understand it is a blunt instrument -- not a
# WAF, useless against a distributed source.
#RATE=0
#BURST=200


# --- Logging -----------------------------------------------------------------

# Suppress the per-request access log. Refusals are still logged.
#QUIET=0
